In article <balaji.748190423@yorku.ca>, balaji@nexus.yorku.ca (Balaji) writes:
>O ye who ascend with a single flick of the wrist:
>
>1) I've already identified an ordinary bag as a "sack"; then I come
>across another "bag", and it doesn't do the bag-of-tricks thing (I'm
>being oh-so-discreet in lieu of the customary ^L) then can I conclude
>it's a bag of holding?
>
No.  It could be a bag of tricks, or an oilskin sack.  To find out if it's
a bag of holding, pick up stuff until your just burdened (eg: many rocks),
and then put a bunch of stuff in the bag (but make sure it's not cursed
first).

>2) Does a "+0 ring of increase damage" do anything? How does one
>increase the enchantment level of rings?
>
I believe a +0 ring does nothing. I don't know how to increase the 
enchantment, I don't really bother with those rings much.  Even if I
found a +5 ring of increase damage, I wouldn't use it.  You only have
two fingers (hands??), and there are many more important rings.

>3) Can nothing change your intelligence? It appears to be so (barring
>helms of brilliance I believe) and if so, the dev.team is taking a
>distinctly reactionary position on genetics versus culture in the debate
>on intelligence.
>
There are many ways to increase intelligence:
 - Blessed potions of gain ability increase all your stats, including
   intelligence.  
 - Eating mind flayers may increase intelligence.
 - Sitting on a throne may increase a random intrinsic.
 - Quaffing from a fountain may increase a random intrinsic
 - Blessed potions of enlightenment increase wisdom and intelligence

>4) Is the ability of priests to tell cursed/blessed status an intrinsic
>that non-priests can acquire?
>
No.  I wish it was (maybe 1/X chance when you eat a temple priest).
